#
# RK3588 AMP board options (sourced via $(DEVICE_PATH)/Kconfig).
#

config RK3588_OPENAMP
    bool "Enable OpenAMP/rpmsg (MICA) support"
    default n
    depends on PLATFORM_RK3588_M
    select COMPONENT_OPENAMP
    help
      Bring up open-amp + libmetal on the RTOS side and register the
      rpmsg-tty endpoint for the Linux MICA framework (/dev/ttyRPMSG0).
      Interrupt notification uses SGI 7 (MICA convention).

config MCS
    bool "Enable MCS hybrid deployment (AMP slave alongside Linux master)"
    default n
    depends on PLATFORM_RK3588_M
    select RK3588_OPENAMP
    help
      MCS hybrid deployment: this core (CPU7, Cortex-A76) runs as the AMP
      slave started and stopped by the Linux master mcs_km driver, sharing
      the debug UART (Linux ttyFIQ0 console) with the master. When enabled:
        - UartInit keeps the shared UART untouched (polled TX only);
        - the shell does not poll UART RX (input comes over rpmsg tty);
        - the mica/rpmsg service task is created at boot.
      When disabled the board owns the UART itself (standalone bring-up).

config MCS_NET
    bool "Real-time NIC data plane on GMAC1 / ETH2 (slave side)"
    default n
    depends on MCS
    help
      Slave-side data plane for the real-time NIC endpoint: the Linux
      master mcs_net.ko gives up GMAC1 (fe1c0000, the ETH2 RJ45 behind
      a YT9214 switch) after bring-up (clocks, reset, pinmux + php_grf/
      sys_grf, PHY/MDIO) and this core runs the RX/TX rings against the
      frozen net.h contract (protocol-agnostic frame driver; any
      link-layer protocol is implemented on top). The GMAC1 interrupt
      (INTID 266 = GIC SPI 234 + 32) is owned by this core and
      registered via ArchHwiCreate. Rx/Tx mode (interrupt / polled /
      hybrid) is a runtime NetConfig option, not a build switch. The
      RGMII side runs fixed 1000M full duplex (the switch buffers
      between port speeds), so there is no forced-link policy. A user
      demo task (net_test.c) is compiled in: polled RX, sending
      numbered 0x88B5 test frames and printing statistics once a
      second.

config MCS_CAN
    bool "Real-time CAN data plane on CAN1/CAN2, classic + CAN-FD (slave side)"
    default n
    depends on MCS
    help
      Slave-side data plane for the real-time CAN endpoints: the Linux
      master mcs_can.ko gives up CAN1 (fea60000) and CAN2 (fea70000)
      after bring-up (baudclk retarget + clock hold, CRU resets,
      can1m1/can2m1 pinmux) and this core runs the tx/rx fifos against
      the can.h v2 contract (classic CAN 2.0B AND CAN-FD frames,
      protocol-agnostic). The CAN interrupts (INTID 374/375 = GIC SPI
      342/343 + 32) are owned by this core and registered via
      ArchHwiCreate. Rx/Tx mode, work mode (loopback/silent), bit rate
      (arbitration + FD data phase) are runtime CanConfig options, not
      build switches. A self-test task (can_test.c) is compiled in:
      loopback phases covering classic frames, FD frames (FDF/BRS, up
      to 64 bytes, data rates 2M/5M), acceptance filtering and both
      ports - zero external hardware required.
